From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 09:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath12k: spread the PCI DP ring interrupts over the CPUs

ath12k on PCI gives the first DP interrupt groups an MSI vector each
when the device grants the full set (8 DP vectors in
ath12k_wifi7_msi_config[]), but leaves their affinity at the default.
Every REO destination ring and TX completion ring interrupt then runs
on the same CPU, and the spread the hardware gives received frames over
the four REO rings is lost before the stack sees them.

Place each REO destination ring interrupt on its own CPU and each TX
completion ring interrupt on the CPU after its ring number, as Julius
Bairaktaris's ath11k patch of the same name (openwrt/openwrt#26253)
does. Only the first groups, one per vector, are placed: the monitor
destination groups share the vectors of the first TX completion groups
and follow them, and with a single shared MSI vector nothing changes.
The affinity hint is cleared before the interrupt is freed.

On an Arista C-460 (IPQ9574, four cores, three QCN9274 hw2.0) all 24 DP
vectors counted on CPU0. With a 320 MHz 6 GHz station (2 streams)
sending TCP to the AP, one core was saturated at 2.79 Gbit/s. With the
patch each radio's vectors sit on CPUs 1,2,3,0 (TX completion rings)
and 0,1,2,3 (REO rings), and seven runs gave 3.02 to 3.67 Gbit/s
(median 3.12); AP transmit went from 1.33 to 1.45-1.89 Gbit/s.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-mythos-5-2
Signed-off-by: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath12k/pci.c
@@ -321,8 +321,12 @@ static void ath12k_pci_free_ext_irq(stru
 	for (i = 0; i < ATH12K_EXT_IRQ_GRP_NUM_MAX; i++) {
 		struct ath12k_ext_irq_grp *irq_grp = &ab->ext_irq_grp[i];
 
-		for (j = 0; j < irq_grp->num_irq; j++)
-			free_irq(ab->irq_num[irq_grp->irqs[j]], irq_grp);
+		for (j = 0; j < irq_grp->num_irq; j++) {
+			int irq = ab->irq_num[irq_grp->irqs[j]];
+
+			irq_update_affinity_hint(irq, NULL);
+			free_irq(irq, irq_grp);
+		}
 
 		netif_napi_del(&irq_grp->napi);
 		free_netdev(irq_grp->napi_ndev);
@@ -538,6 +542,27 @@ static irqreturn_t ath12k_pci_ext_interr
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
 
+static void ath12k_pci_ext_irq_set_affinity(struct ath12k_base *ab,
+					    int grp_id, int irq)
+{
+	const struct ath12k_hw_ring_mask *ring_mask = ab->hw_params->ring_mask;
+	unsigned int cpu;
+
+	/* Put each REO destination ring, over which the hardware hashes
+	 * the received frames, on its own CPU, and each TX completion ring
+	 * on the CPU after its number.
+	 */
+	if (ring_mask->rx[grp_id])
+		cpu = __ffs(ring_mask->rx[grp_id]);
+	else if (ring_mask->tx[grp_id])
+		cpu = __ffs(ring_mask->tx[grp_id]) + 1;
+	else
+		return;
+
+	cpu = cpumask_local_spread(cpu, dev_to_node(ab->dev));
+	irq_set_affinity_and_hint(irq, cpumask_of(cpu));
+}
+
 static int ath12k_pci_ext_irq_config(struct ath12k_base *ab)
 {
 	struct ath12k_pci *ab_pci = ath12k_pci_priv(ab);
@@ -601,6 +626,10 @@ static int ath12k_pci_ext_irq_config(str
 					   vector, ret);
 				goto fail_request;
 			}
+
+			if (test_bit(ATH12K_PCI_FLAG_MULTI_MSI_VECTORS,
+				     &ab_pci->flags) && i < num_vectors)
+				ath12k_pci_ext_irq_set_affinity(ab, i, irq);
 		}
 		ath12k_pci_ext_grp_disable(irq_grp);
 	}
